5
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Treblinka (Polish: [trɛˈbliŋ.ka]; German: [tʁeˈblɪŋka]) was the second-deadliest extermination camp to be built
and operated by Nazi Germany in occupied Poland during World War II, second only to Auschwitz.

6
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:06,000
It was in a forest north-east of Warsaw, four kilometres (2+1⁄2 miles) south of
the village of Treblinka in what is now the Masovian Voivodeship.

7
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:26,000
The camp operated between 23 July 1942 and 19 October 1943 as part of
Operation Reinhard, the deadliest phase of the Final Solution.

8
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
During this time, it is estimated that between 800,000 and 925,000 Jews were
murdered in its gas chambers, along with 2,000 Romani people.

9
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:06,000
More Jews were murdered at Treblinka than at any other Nazi
extermination camp apart from Auschwitz-Birkenau.

10
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:26,000
Managed by the German SS with assistance from Trawniki guards – recruited from among Soviet
POWs to serve with the Germans – the camp consisted of two separate units.

11
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:46,000
Treblinka I was a forced-labour camp (Arbeitslager) whose prisoners worked in the gravel pit or
irrigation area and in the forest, where they cut wood to fuel the cremation pits.

12
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Between 1941 and 1944, more than half of its 20,000 inmates were
murdered via shootings, hunger, disease and mistreatment.

13
00:43:20,000 --> 00:43:26,000
The second camp, Treblinka II, was an extermination camp (Vernichtungslager), referred
to euphemistically as the SS-Sonderkommando Treblinka by the Nazis.

14
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
A small number of Jewish men who were not murdered immediately upon arrival became members of its
Sonderkommando whose jobs included being forced to bury the victims' bodies in mass graves.

15
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:06,000
These bodies were exhumed in 1943 and cremated on large
open-air pyres along with the bodies of new victims.

16
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:26,000
Gassing operations at Treblinka II ended in October 1943
following a revolt by the prisoners in early August.

17
00:56:40,000 --> 00:56:46,000
Several Trawniki guards  were killed and 200 prisoners  escaped from the
camp; almost a hundred survived the subsequent pursuit.

18
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
The camp was dismantled in late 1943.

19
01:03:20,000 --> 01:03:26,000
A farmhouse for a watchman was built on the site and the ground
ploughed over in an attempt to hide the evidence of genocide.

20
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:46,000
In the postwar Polish People's Republic, the government bought most of the land where the
camp had stood, and built a large stone memorial there between 1959 and 1962.

21
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
In 1964, Treblinka was declared a national monument of Jewish martyrdom
in a ceremony at the site of the former gas chambers.

22
01:13:20,000 --> 01:13:26,000
In the same year, the first German trials were held regarding the
crimes committed at Treblinka by former SS members.

23
01:16:40,000 --> 01:16:46,000
After the end of communism in Poland in 1989, the number of
visitors coming to Treblinka from abroad increased.

24
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
An exhibition centre at the camp opened in 2006.

25
01:23:20,000 --> 01:23:26,000
It was later expanded and made into a branch of the Siedlce Regional Museum.

26
01:26:40,000 --> 01:26:46,000
The tone is dark.
